        <p>You've probably heard of heart rate variability — HRV if you're into the jargon — if you've ever worn a fitness tracker or smart watch. Most people know it as something athletes track to optimise performance. But there's a growing body of research connecting HRV to something more personal: your mental health.</p>

        <p>Here's what the science actually says, what it means in practice, and whether tracking HRV is actually useful for managing stress and anxiety.</p>

        <h2>What Is HRV, Exactly?</h2>

        <p>HRV stands for heart rate variability — the variation in time between successive heartbeats. It's not the same as your average heart rate. Even when your heart beats at 70 beats per minute on average, the gap between each beat fluctuates slightly. That's HRV.</p>

        <p>These variations are controlled by your autonomic nervous system (ANS) — the automatic systems you don't consciously control. The sympathetic branch acts like a gas pedal (speeding things up during stress), while the parasympathetic branch acts like a brake (calming things down). HRV is essentially a readout of how well these two systems are talking to each other.</p>

        <p>High HRV generally means your nervous system is adaptable and resilient — you're handling stress well and recovering efficiently. Low HRV can indicate chronic stress, overtraining, poor sleep, or underlying health issues.</p>

        <p>You can measure HRV with various devices, from chest straps to wrist-worn wearables like Garmin, Whoop, Oura, and Apple Watch.</p>

        <h2>The HRV–Mental Health Connection</h2>

        <p>Research over the past decade has consistently found links between low HRV and poor mental health outcomes. The relationship isn't perfectly understood, but several patterns emerge clearly from the literature.</p>

        <h3>Anxiety and Depression</h3>

        <p>Multiple studies have found that people with anxiety disorders and depression tend to show lower HRV than healthy controls. A <a href="https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">2021 meta-analysis in JAMA Psychiatry</a> found robust associations between reduced HRV and both anxiety and depressive disorders, suggesting HRV may serve as a physiological marker for these conditions.</p>

        <p>The direction of the relationship is still debated — does low HRV contribute to anxiety, or does anxiety lower HRV? Most researchers believe it's bidirectional. Chronic anxiety suppresses parasympathetic activity, which reduces HRV. In turn, a low-HRV nervous system may be less able to regulate emotional responses, perpetuating anxiety.</p>

        <h3>Stress and the HPA Axis</h3>

        <p>Your hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis controls cortisol release — your primary stress hormone. Research shows that people with dysregulated HPA axis function (common in chronic stress) often exhibit lower HRV. This connection is why HRV is increasingly used as a proxy for stress load in research and in some clinical settings.</p>

        <h3>Post-Traumatic Stress Disorder (PTSD)</h3>

        <p>Perhaps the most striking HRV findings come from PTSD research. Studies consistently show markedly reduced HRV in individuals with PTSD compared to trauma-exposed controls without PTSD. Some researchers propose that HRV biofeedback — training yourself to increase HRV — may help treat PTSD symptoms by strengthening parasympathetic regulation.</p>

        <h2>Can You Improve Your HRV?</h2>

        <p>The good news: HRV isn't fixed. It responds to lifestyle changes, and some of those changes are well within your control.</p>

        <h3>Breathing and HRV Biofeedback</h3>

        <p>Slow, diaphragmatic breathing at around 5–7 breaths per minute can significantly increase HRV in the short term. This is the basis of HRV biofeedback training, which uses a device to give you real-time feedback on your HRV as you breathe. Research suggests consistent practice can produce lasting changes in autonomic regulation.</p>

        <p>Apps like Elite HRV, Welltory, and even some smartwatch companion apps offer HRV biofeedback exercises. The evidence for long-term benefit is promising but still emerging.</p>

        <h3>Sleep Quality</h3>

        <p>Sleep is one of the most powerful drivers of HRV. Poor sleep — particularly insufficient REM or deep sleep — consistently suppresses HRV the following day. Prioritising sleep hygiene (consistent bedtimes, cool dark room, limiting screen time before bed) is likely the single biggest thing most people can do to improve their HRV.</p>

        <h3>Exercise and Physical Activity</h3>

        <p>Regular aerobic exercise improves HRV over time by enhancing cardiovascular efficiency and parasympathetic tone. Overtraining, however, has the opposite effect — chronic under-recovery suppresses HRV. This is why athletes use HRV to guide training load.</p>

        <p>For non-athletes, moderate consistent exercise (3–5 sessions per week of walking, cycling, swimming, or similar) is associated with healthier HRV profiles.</p>

        <h3>Chronic Stress and Mental Health Support</h3>

        <p>If chronic stress or anxiety is driving low HRV, addressing the root cause matters most. Online therapy platforms like <a href="https://www.betterhelp.com" target="_blank" rel="noopener">BetterHelp</a> connect Australians with licensed therapists who can help develop personalised stress management strategies — often at a fraction of the cost of in-person sessions.</p>

        <h2>Should You Track HRV?</h2>

        <p>Wearable HRV tracking has become extremely accessible — most modern smartwatches and fitness trackers include it. But it's worth having realistic expectations about what the data can and can't tell you.</p>

        <p>HRV is highly individual. Your baseline is unique to you, and what matters most is your own trend over time, not how you compare to others or to population averages. A single low-HRV reading is not clinically meaningful; patterns over weeks and months are more useful.</p>

        <p>That said, HRV tracking can be a useful tool for noticing when your nervous system is under load — before you might consciously feel stressed. If you notice a sustained drop in your HRV alongside poor sleep or increased work pressure, it might be a signal to prioritised recovery before burnout sets in.</p>

