        <h2>The Bottom Line</h2>

        <p>HRV is a genuine window into your autonomic nervous system, and the research linking it to mental health conditions like anxiety and depression is solid. Lower HRV consistently correlates with poorer mental health outcomes across multiple large studies.</p>

        <p>That said, HRV is not a diagnostic tool and shouldn't replace professional mental health assessment. If you're experiencing persistent anxiety, low mood, or signs of burnout, speaking with a therapist is the most evidence-based step you can take.</p>

        <p>For those simply wanting to take a more proactive approach to their wellbeing, HRV tracking — combined with attention to sleep, exercise, and stress management — is a reasonable and increasingly accessible place to start.</p>

